Don't split a command built with -I that is too long for Windows.
When quoting pushes a command line past the Windows limit, xargs runs
the arguments that fit and keeps the rest for the next command. With
-I every argument comes from one input line, and the strings are
allocated per line, so the kept tail made no sense as a command and
was freed when the next line was read: "xargs -I{} sh -c ... {} {}"
with a 20000 byte line ran sh with the first copy only, then ran a
freed string as the program.

With -I, report the line as too long instead, as xargs already does
when an input line does not fit in its buffer.

---
 findutils/xargs.c |    6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/findutils/xargs.c
+++ b/findutils/xargs.c
@@ -946,7 +946,9 @@
 
 #if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
 		/* Check if quoting expands the command line.  If it does we
-		 * truncate args[] and preserve the tail for processing later. */
+		 * truncate args[] and preserve the tail for processing later.
+		 * With -I the args come from a single input line and form one
+		 * command, which can't be split. */
 		args = G.args;
 		if (quote) {
  skip_read:
@@ -954,6 +956,8 @@
 			for (i = initial_idx; args[i]; i++) {
 				n_chars += quote_len(args[i]) + 1;
 				if (n_chars > WIN32_MAX_CHARS) {
+					if (opt & (OPT_REPLSTR | OPT_REPLSTR1))
+						bb_simple_error_msg_and_die("argument line too long");
 					tail = args + i;
 					saved_arg = *tail;
 					*tail = NULL;
